# Confluence Editing — {{ project_name }}

Rules for creating and updating pages in Confluence. Read this before any Confluence
write operation.

## Page Creation

- Always create pages with **Storage Format** (not Markdown or Wiki markup).
- Set the `status` page property immediately — do not leave it as "current".
- The page title **must** contain the ticket key for searchability and cold-start reconstruction.
- Use the correct parent page ID from `.claude/references/project-constants.md`.

## Storage Format vs. Markdown

Confluence's REST API uses Storage Format (XHTML-based), not Markdown. Key differences:

| Markdown | Storage Format equivalent |
|---|---|
| `**bold**` | `<strong>bold</strong>` |
| `# Heading 1` | `<h1>Heading 1</h1>` |
| `\| col1 \| col2 \|` | `<table><tr><th>col1</th>...` |
| ` ```code``` ` | `<code>code</code>` or `<ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">` |

When writing page content via the MCP, always pass `content.representation = "storage"`.

## Updating Existing Pages

Before updating a page:
1. Fetch the current page to get the `version.number`.
2. Increment `version.number` by 1 in your update request — Confluence will reject stale updates.
3. Preserve the `status` property unless you are intentionally changing it (e.g. In Review → Approved).

## Status Field

Use the Confluence page properties macro to maintain the `status` field:

| Status | When to set it |
|---|---|
| `In Review` | test-design-agent publishes the TD doc |
| `Approved` | bdd-agent promotes the TD doc after publishing cases |
| `Deprecated` | Feature is removed or superseded |

## Search Tips

To find a page by ticket key:
```
CQL: title ~ "PROJ-1234" AND space = "<SPACE_KEY>"
```

Use `searchConfluenceUsingCql` with this query before creating a new page — always
check for an existing page first.

## Hard Rules

- **Never** create a duplicate page — search before creating.
- **Never** use Markdown format for page content — always use Storage Format.
- **Never** omit the `version.number` on an update — the request will fail.
- **Never** delete a page — deprecate it by setting the status property to `Deprecated`.
